{"decisions":[{"id":"c8","verdict":"keep","level":"E2","same_as":"","reason":"The question 'Can I see Mr. Cooper, your manager now?' presupposes that B (Mrs. White) has the role or ability to facilitate or grant access to Mr. Cooper. This is a standard pragmatic presupposition of such requests directed at a specific person—A would not ask B unless B had some capacity to arrange or permit the meeting. The presupposition is minimal and justified by the question structure itself.","counterexample":"If B were merely a bystander with no connection to Mr. Cooper's scheduling, the question would be pragmatically odd, supporting the presupposition."},{"id":"c9","verdict":"reject","level":"E4","same_as":"","reason":"Turn 5 'All right' is explicitly A's own utterance (visible_turns lists it as A's turn). Attributing agreement or an affirmative response to B requires inferring an unseen B reply. No visible turn from B confirms any response to A's request. This violates the principle that we must not invent or assume B's replies.","counterexample":"A could have said 'All right' after B said 'No, he's unavailable,' making the attribution of agreement to B clearly wrong."}]}
```

